= Solution
{parent=2}

Write $m_0=\mu_0\rho$ and treat the imposed mean <magnetic field> as uniform on the forcing scale. In <first-order smoothing>, retain terms linear in the fluctuating fields in their equations, while keeping their quadratic correlation when calculating the <mean-field electromotive force>. The inertial fluctuation, nonlinear induction term and quadratic fluctuating <Lorentz force density> are neglected in the steady response. The linear magnetic force is
$$
\frac1{m_0}(\nabla\times\mathbf b)\times\mathbf B
=\frac1{m_0}(\mathbf B\cdot\nabla)\mathbf b-\nabla\frac{\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf b}{m_0}.
$$
Absorb the gradient into the total pressure. The resulting equations are
$$
0=-\rho^{-1}\nabla P+m_0^{-1}(\mathbf B\cdot\nabla)\mathbf b+\nu\nabla^2\mathbf u+\mathbf f,
\qquad
0=(\mathbf B\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u+\eta\nabla^2\mathbf b+\mathbf g,
$$
with $\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=\nabla\cdot\mathbf b=0$. Taking the divergence of the first equation gives $\nabla^2P=0$ for solenoidal forcing. In a periodic or homogeneous Fourier calculation its nonconstant part therefore vanishes.

For each <Fourier mode> with wavevector $\mathbf q$, $|\mathbf q|=k$, define $\ell=\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf q$ and
$$
D=\nu\eta k^4+\frac{\ell^2}{m_0}>0.
$$
Solving the two coupled linear equations gives the <monochromatic coupled magnetic and velocity response>
$$
\boxed{\widehat{\mathbf u}=\frac{\eta k^2\widehat{\mathbf f}+i\ell\widehat{\mathbf g}/m_0}{D},
\qquad
\widehat{\mathbf b}=\frac{\nu k^2\widehat{\mathbf g}+i\ell\widehat{\mathbf f}}{D}.}
$$
The magnetic coupling suppresses modes with large projection of their wavevector along the imposed field.

To calculate the <mean-field electromotive force>, group each pair of opposite wavevectors into a real mode and average its spatial phase. Put $\mathbf n=\mathbf q/k$, and define its forcing correlations
$$
\mathbf C_{\mathbf q}=\langle\mathbf f_{\mathbf q}\times\mathbf g_{\mathbf q}\rangle,
\quad
h_f(\mathbf q)=\langle\mathbf f_{\mathbf q}\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf f_{\mathbf q}\rangle,
\quad
h_g(\mathbf q)=\langle\mathbf g_{\mathbf q}\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf g_{\mathbf q}\rangle.
$$
For any solenoidal real mode $\mathbf v=\mathbf a\cos(\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf x)+\mathbf c\sin(\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf x)$, its two amplitude vectors are perpendicular to $\mathbf q$. Direct multiplication gives
$$
\langle\mathbf v\times(\mathbf B\cdot\nabla)\mathbf v\rangle
=-\mathbf n(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf B)\langle\mathbf v\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf v\rangle.
$$
Also, integration by parts gives $\langle(L\mathbf g)\times(L\mathbf f)\rangle=-\ell^2\langle\mathbf f\times\mathbf g\rangle$, where $L=\mathbf B\cdot\nabla$. Expanding the response products therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}_{\mathbf q}
=\frac{\nu\eta k^4-\ell^2/m_0}{D^2}\mathbf C_{\mathbf q}
+\frac{k^2}{D^2}\mathbf n(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf B)
\left(\frac{\nu}{m_0}h_g(\mathbf q)-\eta h_f(\mathbf q)\right).}
$$
Summing over independent real modes, or integrating over their angular spectrum, is the required general answer for $\langle\mathbf u\times\mathbf b\rangle$.

At zero field, $\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}(0)=\sum_{\mathbf q}\mathbf C_{\mathbf q}/(\nu\eta k^4)$. Thus the stated zero-field condition removes the unweighted sum of the cross correlations. For anisotropic correlated forcing it does not necessarily remove their differently weighted sum at nonzero field. The displayed general formula retains that allowed contribution. It disappears if cross forcing is absent mode by mode, or under the jointly isotropic statistics assumed for the final part.

The helicity content can also be expressed directly using the actual fluctuating fields. Define their <kinetic helicity> $H_u(\mathbf q)=\langle\mathbf u_{\mathbf q}\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf u_{\mathbf q}\rangle$ and <current helicity> $H_b(\mathbf q)=\langle\mathbf b_{\mathbf q}\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf b_{\mathbf q}\rangle$. Substitute $\mathbf f=\nu k^2\mathbf u-L\mathbf b/m_0$ and $\mathbf g=\eta k^2\mathbf b-L\mathbf u$ into their cross correlation. This gives the exact identity
$$
\left(\nu\eta k^4-\frac{\ell^2}{m_0}\right)\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}_{\mathbf q}-\mathbf C_{\mathbf q}
=k^2\mathbf n(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf B)
\left(\frac{\eta}{m_0}H_b(\mathbf q)-\nu H_u(\mathbf q)\right).
$$
It shows the competing kinetic and magnetic contributions without introducing a spurious singularity when the factor on the left vanishes. For a monochromatic field, $\mathbf a_b=\nabla\times\mathbf b/k^2$ is a <magnetic vector potential>, so $H_b=k^2\langle\mathbf a_b\cdot\mathbf b\rangle$: <current helicity> is $k^2$ times <magnetic helicity>. In the isotropic weak-field limit, with zero-field fluctuations $\mathbf u_0,\mathbf b_0$, the scalar <alpha effect> is
$$
\alpha(0)=-\frac{\langle\mathbf u_0\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf u_0\rangle}{3\eta k^2}
+\frac{\langle\mathbf b_0\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf b_0\rangle}{3\nu m_0 k^2}.
$$
This identifies both signs and their different viscous and magnetic relaxation times.

For jointly isotropic forcing let $h_f,h_g$ now denote the total forcing helicities, and set $S_h=\nu h_g/m_0-\eta h_f$. The cross-forcing contribution is even in $\mathbf B$, whereas isotropy requires any vector depending only on $\mathbf B$ to be parallel to it and odd under reversal. Hence that contribution vanishes. A natural symmetric <alpha tensor> is
$$
\alpha_{ij}^{\mathrm{nat}}=k^2S_h\left\langle\frac{n_in_j}{[\nu\eta k^4+k^2|B|^2\mu^2/m_0]^2}\right\rangle_{\mathbf n},
\qquad \mu=\mathbf n\cdot\widehat{\mathbf B}.
$$
Only its longitudinal coefficient contributes to $\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}=\alpha_{\parallel}\mathbf B$. Introduce $h=|B|/[k\sqrt{m_0\nu\eta}]$ and $A_0=\nu\eta k^4$. Angular integration gives
$$
\alpha_{\parallel}=\frac{k^2S_h}{A_0^2}\int_0^1\frac{\mu^2d\mu}{(1+h^2\mu^2)^2}
=\frac{k^2S_h}{2A_0^2h^3}\left(\arctan h-\frac{h}{1+h^2}\right).
$$
Therefore the <strong-field quenching of an isotropic electromotive force> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha_{\parallel}\sim\frac{\pi k^2S_h}{4A_0^2h^3}\propto |B|^{-3},\qquad
|\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}|\propto |B|^{-2},}
$$
provided $S_h\ne0$. If the two helicity contributions cancel exactly, this electromotive force vanishes.

There is a tensor qualification to the printed scaling claim. The natural transverse coefficient instead contains $\tfrac12\int_0^1(1-\mu^2)(1+h^2\mu^2)^{-2}d\mu\sim\pi/(8h)$ and scales as $|B|^{-1}$. The relation $\mathcal E_i=\alpha_{ij}B_j$ by itself leaves transverse components undetermined. One may choose the effective representative $\alpha_{ij}=\alpha_{\parallel}\delta_{ij}$, for which the requested $|B|^{-3}$ scaling holds. It is the observable longitudinal response that is fixed, rather than every component of a unique field-dependent tensor.

= 3
{parent=Paper 67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Let the layer depth be $d$, its imposed temperature drop $\Delta T$, and its dimensional vertical field $B_0\widehat{\mathbf z}$. Use length $d$, thermal time $d^2/\kappa$, velocity $\kappa/d$, temperature $\Delta T$ and field $B_0$ as units. Write the total dimensionless field as $\widehat{\mathbf z}+\mathbf b$, and temperature as the conductive profile plus $\theta$. The <Boussinesq equations> coupled to the <resistive induction equation> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\partial_t\mathbf u+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla\mathbf u
&=-\nabla p+\sigma\nabla^2\mathbf u+\sigma R\theta\widehat{\mathbf z}
+\sigma Q(\nabla\times\mathbf b)\times(\widehat{\mathbf z}+\mathbf b),\\
\partial_t\theta+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla\theta&=w+\nabla^2\theta,\\
\partial_t\mathbf b&=\nabla\times[\mathbf u\times(\widehat{\mathbf z}+\mathbf b)]+\zeta\nabla^2\mathbf b,\\
\nabla\cdot\mathbf u&=\nabla\cdot\mathbf b=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
The dimensionless parameters in this normalization are
$$
\boxed{R=\frac{g\alpha_T\Delta T\,d^3}{\nu\kappa},\qquad
\sigma=\frac\nu\kappa,\qquad\zeta=\frac\eta\kappa,\qquad
Q=\frac{B_0^2d^2}{\mu_0\rho\nu\kappa}.}
$$
Here $\alpha_T$ is the thermal expansion coefficient, $\nu$ is <kinematic viscosity>, $\kappa$ is <thermal diffusivity>, and $\eta$ is <magnetic diffusivity>. The first parameter is the <Rayleigh number> and $\sigma$ is the <Prandtl number>.

The displayed dispersion relation fixes the magnetic normalization: its $Q$ must be the <thermal-diffusion magnetic-field parameter> above. The conventional <Chandrasekhar number> is instead $Q_{\mathrm{Ch}}=B_0^2d^2/(\mu_0\rho\nu\eta)$, so $Q=\zeta Q_{\mathrm{Ch}}$. If $Q$ is reserved for that conventional definition, the magnetic term in the printed polynomial requires an extra factor $\zeta$. We keep $Q$ for the printed polynomial throughout the following calculation.

Linearize about the conductive state. Absorbing linear magnetic pressure into $p$, the magnetic force becomes $\sigma Q\partial_z\mathbf b$. The pressure-eliminated vertical momentum equation is
$$
(\partial_t-\sigma\nabla^2)\nabla^2w
=\sigma R\nabla_h^2\theta+\sigma Q\partial_z\nabla^2b_z.
$$
The normal mode implicit in the given relation has $w,\theta\propto\sin\pi z$ and $b_z\propto\cos\pi z$, as for stress-free, fixed-temperature boundaries with the corresponding vertical-field magnetic conditions. Put $x=k^2$, $p_0=\pi^2$ and $m=x+p_0=\beta^2$. Taking growth proportional to $e^{st+i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x_h}$ gives the amplitude equations
$$
(s+m)\Theta=W,\qquad(s+\zeta m)C=\pi W,
\qquad
m(s+\sigma m)W=\sigma R x\Theta-\sigma Qm\pi C.
$$
Their determinant is
$$
\boxed{m(s+m)(s+\sigma m)(s+\zeta m)+\sigma Qmp_0(s+m)-R\sigma x(s+\zeta m)=0.}
$$
This is the stated <vertical-field magnetoconvection dispersion relation> after accounting for its field-strength convention. The determinant derivation remains valid even at values where an intermediate division by $s+m$ or $s+\zeta m$ would not be allowed.

For steady marginality set $s=0$. One obtains
$$
\boxed{R_s(x)=\frac{m^3+(Qp_0/\zeta)m}{x}.}
$$
For oscillatory marginality write $s=i\omega$ with $\omega\ne0$. Dividing the polynomial by $m$ gives a real cubic $s^3+A s^2+B s+C_0$, where
$$
A=m(1+\sigma+\zeta),\quad
B=m^2(\sigma+\zeta+\sigma\zeta)+\sigma Qp_0-\frac{R\sigma x}{m},
$$
$$
C_0=\sigma\zeta m^3+\sigma Qp_0m-R\sigma\zeta x.
$$
Its real and imaginary parts imply $\omega^2=B$ and $C_0=AB$. Solving for the <oscillatory convection> threshold gives
$$
\boxed{R_o(x)=\frac{(\sigma+\zeta)(1+\zeta)}{\sigma}\frac{m^3}{x}
+\frac{\sigma+\zeta}{1+\sigma}\frac{Qp_0m}{x},}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{\omega^2=\frac{\sigma(1-\zeta)}{1+\sigma}Qp_0-\zeta^2m^2.}
$$
A physical oscillatory branch therefore requires $0<\zeta<1$ and positive frequency squared. In particular an algebraic continuation of $R_o$ into $\omega^2<0$ is not an oscillatory onset.

The <steady-Hopf merger in vertical-field magnetoconvection> occurs at zero frequency:
$$
\boxed{Qp_0=\frac{\zeta^2(1+\sigma)}{\sigma(1-\zeta)}m^2.}
$$
Substitution verifies $R_s=R_o$. Equivalently the merged horizontal wavenumber satisfies
$$
k_m^2=\left[\frac{\sigma(1-\zeta)Qp_0}{\zeta^2(1+\sigma)}\right]^{1/2}-p_0.
$$
A finite-wavenumber intersection exists only when this expression is positive. At the intersection the growth polynomial has a double zero root: the nonzero-frequency neutral branch ends there.

For the requested <merger at a magnetoconvection neutral-curve minimum>, first note that a curve of the form $R=[A_*m^3+C_*m]/x$ has derivative zero exactly when
$$
A_*m^2(2x-p_0)=C_*p_0.
$$
Its unique positive minimum has $x>p_0/2$, because the left side increases there from zero to infinity. At this minimum $R_c=2A_*m^3/p_0$.

**Merger at the steady minimum.** For $R_s$, the stationarity condition is $m_s^2(2x_s-p_0)=Qp_0^2/\zeta$. Combining it with the merger equation gives
$$
\boxed{x_s=\frac{p_0(\sigma+\zeta)}{2\sigma(1-\zeta)},\qquad
Q_s=\frac{p_0\zeta^2(1+\sigma)(3\sigma-2\sigma\zeta+\zeta)^2}{4\sigma^3(1-\zeta)^3}.}
$$
The corresponding critical Rayleigh number is $R_{s,c}=2(x_s+p_0)^3/p_0$. For fixed positive $\sigma$ and large $Q$, set $\zeta=1-\delta_s$ and retain the leading powers. Then
$$
\boxed{\delta_s\sim\frac{1+\sigma}{\sigma}\left(\frac{\pi^2}{4Q}\right)^{1/3},\qquad
k_s\sim\left(\frac{Q\pi^4}{2}\right)^{1/6},\qquad R_{s,c}\sim\pi^2Q.}
$$
More generally, before taking $\zeta\to1$, the steady minimum obeys $k_s^2\sim[Q\pi^4/(2\zeta)]^{1/3}$.

**Merger at the oscillatory minimum.** Define $A_o=(\sigma+\zeta)(1+\zeta)/\sigma$. The stationarity condition for $R_o$ is
$$
m_o^2(2x_o-p_0)=\frac{\sigma Qp_0^2}{(1+\sigma)(1+\zeta)}.
$$
Combining it with the zero-frequency relation instead gives
$$
\boxed{x_o=\frac{p_0}{2(1-\zeta^2)},\qquad
Q_o=\frac{p_0\zeta^2(1+\sigma)(3-2\zeta^2)^2}{4\sigma(1-\zeta)^3(1+\zeta)^2}.}
$$
Here $R_{o,c}=2A_o(x_o+p_0)^3/p_0$. For fixed $\sigma>0$ and $Q\gg1$, setting $\zeta=1-\delta_o$ gives
$$
\boxed{\delta_o\sim\left[\frac{(1+\sigma)\pi^2}{16\sigma Q}\right]^{1/3},\qquad
k_o\sim\left[\frac{\sigma Q\pi^4}{4(1+\sigma)}\right]^{1/6},\qquad R_{o,c}\sim\pi^2Q.}
$$
These two merger conditions are distinct parameter surfaces, not simultaneous assertions about the same minimum. Both have the <strong-field wavenumber selection in magnetoconvection> scaling $k=O(Q^{1/6})$.

If the first subleading Rayleigh corrections are desired, let $X_s=[Qp_0^2/(2\zeta)]^{1/3}$ and $X_o=[\sigma Qp_0^2/(2(1+\sigma)(1+\zeta))]^{1/3}$. With parameters bounded in the above regime,
$$
R_{s,c}=\frac{Qp_0}{\zeta}+3X_s^2+O(Q^{1/3}),\qquad
R_{o,c}=\frac{\sigma+\zeta}{1+\sigma}Qp_0+3A_oX_o^2+O(Q^{1/3}).
$$
These retain the $O(Q^{2/3})$ corrections, including the effect of the $O(Q^{-1/3})$ diffusivity deficit at a merger.

= 4
{parent=Paper 67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

A <planetary magnetic field> is evidence about electrical conductivity, fluid motion and thermal evolution, but its presence alone does not identify a present <planetary dynamo>. One must separate internal global fields, crustal <remanent magnetization> and currents driven by the <solar wind>. The following discussion uses the observational picture available by 2009, including the early MESSENGER flybys and the Voyager, Galileo and Cassini results.

Outside a planet's internal electric currents, the internal component is approximately a potential <magnetic field>. Its <spherical harmonic> of degree $\ell$ decreases as $r^{-(\ell+2)}$. Measurements at different positions and altitudes can therefore distinguish a deep, low-degree source from localized crustal anomalies and external magnetospheric currents. Repeated observations, radio emissions, aurorae and particle measurements supply complementary evidence for a <magnetosphere>, while slow secular variation and records of past reversals test the internal field's evolution.

For <Earth>, ground observatories and satellite measurements reveal a predominantly dipolar field of several tens of microtesla at the surface, tilted relative to the rotation axis and changing with time. Magnetic minerals in rocks record a much older field and repeated <geomagnetic reversals>. These observations, together with seismic evidence for a liquid outer core, support an operating <planetary dynamo> in conducting iron alloy. Core temperatures exceed the relevant <Curie temperatures>, so ordinary permanent <remanent magnetization> cannot explain the main field. Thermal <convection>, cooling and <compositional convection> associated with inner-core growth supply buoyancy; the <Coriolis force> organizes the motion. The observed nonaxisymmetric components and time dependence are consistent with a dynamic source, rather than a fixed bar magnet.

Mariner 10 and the first two MESSENGER flybys established a weak but coherent intrinsic field at <Mercury>, with roughly dipolar structure closely aligned with its rotation axis. It is much weaker than Earth's surface field, but its global character favors a deep source over an explanation solely by localized crustal patches. A <planetary dynamo> in a fluid part of Mercury's large iron-rich core is the natural interpretation, supported by evidence for a molten core component. Light elements can depress the alloy's freezing temperature, and thermal or compositional buoyancy can maintain motion. Slow rotation is not enough to rule out <dynamo action>. The limited pre-orbital measurements do not uniquely determine the detailed core structure or the mechanism responsible for the field's weakness.

For <Venus>, spacecraft including Pioneer Venus found no appreciable internally generated global field. Nevertheless its ionosphere interacts with the <solar wind>, producing magnetic draping, a bow shock and an <induced magnetosphere>. Those external signatures are not evidence for an internal <planetary dynamo>. Venus is comparable to Earth in size and may possess a substantial conducting core; the absence of a detectable dynamo therefore need not mean that all of its core is solid. Insufficient core heat loss or stable stratification can prevent the required <convection>. Its slow rotation can affect flow organization, but rotation rate by itself is not a sufficient explanation of the contrast with Earth or Mercury.

For <Mars>, Mars Global Surveyor found strong, spatially localized crustal anomalies, especially in ancient southern terrain, rather than an appreciable present global dipole. This is <remanent magnetization>, indicating that an ancient field magnetized the crust while it formed and cooled. The distribution of anomalies and weakly magnetized major impact basins constrain when that ancient <planetary dynamo> operated; crustal magnetization is a record of past activity, not a present global dynamo. Modern interaction with the <solar wind> also produces an <induced magnetosphere>. Cooling and changes in core heat extraction can explain the cessation of dynamo-driving <convection>; the observations alone do not prove complete solidification of the core.

For <Jupiter>, radio emissions, its intense radiation belts and measurements by Pioneer, Voyager and Galileo demonstrate a strong global intrinsic field and an enormous <magnetosphere>. The field is broadly dipolar but tilted and contains higher multipoles. It is generated in a deep conducting fluid rather than by a permanent solid magnet: the principal conducting region is <metallic hydrogen> in the high-pressure interior. Cooling and gravitational energy release maintain <convection>, while rapid rotation organizes the flow. Plasma supplied by Io and currents in the <magnetosphere> strongly modify the observed external field, but cannot account for the internally sourced planetary component. Jupiter's large magnetic moment also reflects its large size; a moment ratio should not be confused with the ratio of local surface field strengths.

For <Saturn>, Pioneer, Voyager and Cassini measurements likewise show an intrinsic field and a <magnetosphere>, with a dipole strikingly close to the rotation axis. A <planetary dynamo> in conducting <metallic hydrogen> is supported by the same broad interior argument as for Jupiter. Interior cooling and differentiation can provide power, including energy associated with helium separation. The near alignment does not invalidate the <Cowling anti-dynamo theorem>: that theorem excludes an exactly axisymmetric isolated dynamo field, not a predominantly axisymmetric exterior with undetected nonaxisymmetric structure in its source region. A stably stratified conducting layer above the active region is one possible way to attenuate the observable nonaxisymmetric components; it is a model, not a uniquely established consequence of the observations.

For <Uranus>, Voyager 2 in 1986 observed an intrinsic field with a dipole tilted by about $59^\circ$ to the rotation axis and significantly displaced from the centre. Higher multipoles are substantial, so a centred dipole is an inadequate description. The field and associated <magnetosphere> indicate an active <planetary dynamo>, despite the planet's small intrinsic heat flux. That low luminosity constrains its thermal evolution and may indicate inhibited deep mixing; it does not demonstrate an absence of all conducting-fluid motion. A conducting, volatile-rich fluid shell is a plausible source region. The term <ice giant> describes bulk composition, not a claim that the dynamo region is ordinary frozen ice.

For <Neptune>, Voyager 2 in 1989 also found a highly non-dipolar, displaced intrinsic field, with a dipole tilt near $47^\circ$. Its field, radio emissions and <magnetosphere> imply another active <planetary dynamo>. Its larger intrinsic heat flux than Uranus supplies evidence for substantial continuing interior energy transport. Electrically conducting water-ammonia-rich mixtures at high pressure, with ionic or otherwise mobile charges, can supply the conducting region without requiring the <metallic hydrogen> layer characteristic of <gas giants>. Numerical dynamos in convecting shells surrounding more stably stratified interiors can produce strongly nonaxisymmetric, multipolar fields resembling those of the two <ice giants>. Such models show that their unusual field geometry is compatible with <dynamo action>, but field measurements do not uniquely identify the shell's composition or thickness.

The maintenance mechanism is governed by the <resistive induction equation>,
$$
\partial_t\mathbf B=\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\mathbf B)+\eta\nabla^2\mathbf B,
\qquad
\operatorname{Rm}=\frac{UL}{\eta},\qquad
\eta=\frac1{\mu_0\sigma_e}.
$$
A conducting fluid needs a sufficiently large <magnetic Reynolds number> and a flow geometry that regenerates magnetic flux rather than merely amplifying one component temporarily. Three-dimensional stretching and twisting can couple poloidal and toroidal fields; strictly planar regeneration and a strictly axisymmetric magnetic field are restricted by the relevant <anti-dynamo theorems>. Rotation influences this regeneration through the <Coriolis force>, but does not provide an inexhaustible power source merely by being present.

Without regeneration, the characteristic <magnetic diffusion> time is $\tau_\eta\sim L^2/\eta$. For a terrestrial core scale of order $10^6$ metres and diffusivity of order one square metre per second, this is of order $10^4$–$10^5$ years, far shorter than geological ages. Long-lived global fields therefore require continual energy input. A schematic <magnetic energy> balance is
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\int\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}\,dV
=-\int\mathbf u\cdot(\mathbf J\times\mathbf B)\,dV
-\int\frac{J^2}{\sigma_e}\,dV,
$$
when boundary energy fluxes vanish. Mechanical work against the <Lorentz force density> supplies the energy lost as <Ohmic heating>. Thermal cooling, <latent heat> release, <compositional convection> and differentiation supply that mechanical power. Magnetic feedback eventually changes the flow and saturates the field. Strong conductivity without an adequate energy source is not sufficient for a dynamo.

**The 2009 observations support active global dynamos in Mercury, Earth, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune; Venus has no detected appreciable intrinsic dynamo field, and Mars preserves evidence of an extinct earlier dynamo in its crust.** Induced fields and surviving remanence explain why planets without a current global dynamo can still display magnetic phenomena. The diversity of field geometries and strengths constrains, rather than uniquely solves, the diversity of planetary conducting regions and their convective histories.
